course: dictionary
lesson: 10383
title: Treetops and high places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A treetop is the highest part of a tree, its top branches and leaves. When you meet a new word, look for clues that tell you where something is. Here the clues are words like high, over and up.
explain: «Brachiosaurus had long front legs and could reach treetops about 12 metres up.» The words about 12 metres up tell you treetops are very high. Try swapping in "the highest branches" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «The noctule flies high and fast over the treetops.» Over tells you the bat is above the treetops, not inside them. Small words like over, under and inside often give you the position of a thing.
fact: A treetop is the highest part of a tree: its top branches and leaves.
fact: The treetops form the canopy, where many birds, monkeys and insects live.
fact: «Brachiosaurus» could reach treetops about 12 metres up.
fact: «Paraceratherium» ate leaves from treetops and was the biggest land mammal ever.
fact: Treetop walks let people walk on high bridges between trees, with harnesses for safety.
exercise: «Brachiosaurus could reach treetops about 12 metres up.» Where are the treetops? | *High up, at the top of the trees | On the floor of the rainforest | Deep under the ground
exercise: Which animals live in the canopy, the treetops of a rainforest? | *Birds, monkeys and insects | Fish and whales | Penguins and seals
exercise: «The noctule flies high and fast over the treetops.» Where does the noctule fly? | Inside the trunk of a tree | *Over the top of the trees, high in the air | Under the roots of the trees
test: «The giraffe browses at treetop height, the elephant lower down.» Which animal eats at treetop height? | Zebra | *Giraffe | Meerkat | Elephant
test: Treetop walks let people walk on high bridges between trees. Why do these walks have harnesses? | *To keep people safe | To make people fly | To make the trees grow faster
exam: A treetop is best described as: | The lowest branches, near the roots | *The highest part of a tree, its top branches and leaves | A kind of animal that lives in the canopy | The bark on the trunk
